WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:04.528
- [Narrator] It's April,
1996, and it's time for lunch.

00:00:04.528 --> 00:00:06.360
(upbeat music)

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:07.810
Bill Clinton is the president,

00:00:07.810 --> 00:00:09.510
Celine Dion's "Because You Love Me"

00:00:09.510 --> 00:00:12.020
is on its fifth week at
number one on the Billboards,

00:00:12.020 --> 00:00:15.016
and "Tremors 2" is currently in theaters.

00:00:15.016 --> 00:00:16.470
(school bell rings)
The bell just rang,

00:00:16.470 --> 00:00:19.840
and lunchtime, for you,
might have looked like this.

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:21.500
And if not for you,
then for that other kid

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:23.100
who always got the good lunches.

00:00:24.165 --> 00:00:26.748
(dreamy music)

00:00:28.552 --> 00:00:31.067
♪ Ah ♪

00:00:31.067 --> 00:00:33.193
♪ The meal ♪

00:00:33.193 --> 00:00:35.050
(phone beeping)

00:00:35.050 --> 00:00:36.100
- [Narrator] Bob, can you hear me?

00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:38.320
- I can.
- Bob Drane was the lead

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:40.995
on the team that developed Lunchables.

00:00:40.995 --> 00:00:43.550
- [Bob] It was a real blast,
from beginning to end.

00:00:43.550 --> 00:00:45.970
- [Narrator] But the name,
he says, didn't come easily.

00:00:45.970 --> 00:00:47.557
- [Bob] So we had a bunch of names.

00:00:47.557 --> 00:00:49.087
"Little Oscar's Lunch Fixing's."

00:00:49.087 --> 00:00:51.497
"Cracker Wiches," "Go
Packs," "Snackables,"

00:00:51.497 --> 00:00:53.910
"Mini Meals," "Lunch Express."

00:00:53.910 --> 00:00:55.550
- [Narrator] Until they landed on a name

00:00:55.550 --> 00:00:58.013
that truly defined a generation.

00:00:58.013 --> 00:01:00.075
- [Bob] We ended up with "Lunchables."

00:01:00.075 --> 00:01:03.504
(marker rasping)
(dreamy music)

00:01:03.504 --> 00:01:05.600
♪ The drink ♪

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:07.460
- [Narrator] Okay, if
we had to pick one word

00:01:07.460 --> 00:01:09.130
that defined '90s drinks,

00:01:09.130 --> 00:01:11.240
it would probably be squeezable.

00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:12.800
Between Kool-Aid Bursts, Capri Suns,

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:16.490
Squeezeits, and Mondo,
there was a lot of market.

00:01:16.490 --> 00:01:18.400
- [Ken] They all kind of
came out at the same time.

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:19.670
- [Narrator] That's Ken.

00:01:19.670 --> 00:01:20.590
- [Ken] Hi, It's Ken Wegner.

00:01:20.590 --> 00:01:21.860
- [Narrator] President at Jel Sert,

00:01:21.860 --> 00:01:23.370
the company that made Mondo.

00:01:23.370 --> 00:01:25.960
First question, we wanna
know about the cap.

00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:28.950
- [Ken] Well, it was a
child-protective cap.

00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:30.400
It couldn't be swallowed.

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:31.760
- [Narrator] Right, I
guess when I was younger

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:35.120
it just seemed way cooler
than an anti-choking design.

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:37.380
- [Ken] But it was really
all about fun and flavor,

00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:40.313
and I think kind of, in some
ways, a legendary product.

00:01:42.632 --> 00:01:44.750
♪ The dessert ♪

00:01:44.750 --> 00:01:45.730
- [Narrator] A quick side note:

00:01:45.730 --> 00:01:47.740
Dunkaroos are no longer in production.

00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:50.490
And try as we might, and we tried hard,

00:01:50.490 --> 00:01:53.210
we could not find any, anywhere, for sale.

00:01:53.210 --> 00:01:56.430
So this, it's just a
printout, we're sorry.

00:01:56.430 --> 00:01:58.373
- Aw!
- Okay, back to the script.

00:01:59.370 --> 00:02:02.020
Dunkaroos were king in the lunchroom.

00:02:02.020 --> 00:02:04.060
And the ads were iconic.

00:02:04.060 --> 00:02:08.490
- [John] I paid my bills, in
the mid-90s, with voiceovers.

00:02:08.490 --> 00:02:10.040
- [Narrator] That's John Cameron Mitchell,

00:02:10.040 --> 00:02:12.010
writer of "Hedwig and the Angry Inch,"

00:02:12.010 --> 00:02:13.657
and you might recognize him from "Girls,"

00:02:13.657 --> 00:02:16.011
"Shrill," or "Mozart in the Jungle."

00:02:16.011 --> 00:02:17.130
- But now, new--

00:02:17.130 --> 00:02:18.070
- [Narrator] He was the kangaroo

00:02:18.070 --> 00:02:20.180
in the Dunkaroos commercial.

00:02:20.180 --> 00:02:24.160
- [John] I basically kind
of imitated Robin Leach.

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:26.240
You know, "Lifestyles
of the Rich and Famous."

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:28.860
So I did a very bad Australian accent;

00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:32.050
when I said, "How do
you do your Dunkaroos?"

00:02:32.050 --> 00:02:34.420
I later worked with Nicole
Kidman in "Juniper,"

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:35.968
and she's like not very good.

00:02:35.968 --> 00:02:37.780
- [Crowd] Aw!

00:02:37.780 --> 00:02:39.480
- [Narrator] And there it is, your lunch

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:42.317
and some of the people behind it in 1996.

00:02:42.317 --> 00:02:43.400
- [Kids] Yay!

00:02:47.139 --> 00:02:49.722
(bright music)

